  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nA shallow cold airmass behind a cold front across the northern\nGreat Plains has slowly crept into northeast Nebraska. Low\nclouds have kept temperatures in the 50s behind the front while\ntemperatures are soaring through the 80s to even low 90s over \nthe remainder of the area. It is expected that much of the area \nwill experience one more day of upper 80s and low 90s heat \nbefore a cooling trend takes over once the cold front pushes \nthrough the remainder of the area this evening.\n\nAs a large, upper-level low pressure system moves into the Great\nBasin this evening, weak lee cyclogenesis takes place over\neastern Colorado. This developing feature and the cessation of\ndaytime heating helps push the cold front south of the area.\nBehind the front, cooler temperatures fill in, ranging from the\n40s over northeast Nebraska to upper 50s to near 60 over\nsoutheast Nebraska and southwest Iowa by morning. A few showers\nor isolated thunderstorm may develop along the cold front \novernight with modest warm advection atop the front. This threat\nis low with only light amounts anticipated.\n\nAs the upper-low slowly moves east towards the Four Corners\nregion, surface troughing keeps the cold front nearly stationary\njust south of the area on Tuesday. While portions of southeast \nNebraska and southwest Iowa may warm into the 80s just behind \nthe front, most of the area remains much cooler in the 60s to \nmid-70s. By late afternoon and evening, a leading wave \nassociated with remnants of Rachel will help to initiate \nwidespread showers and thunderstorms across much of the Central \nPlains. The northern extent of this activity may clip far \nsoutheast Nebraska and southwest Iowa along and just behind the \nstalled front. A low chance of strong to severe thunderstorms \nwill exist, but the bulk of severe weather should remain to the \nsouth, ahead of the front. That said, an isolated damaging wind \ngust or large hail can't be ruled out. Rain and thunderstorms \nmay linger into Wednesday near the front, but much of the area \nshould remain dry.\n\nBy mid-week, cooler temperatures in the 60s fully settle into\nthe entire region. At the same time, the upper-low begins to\nmove out into the Great Plains bringing more widespread shower \nand thunderstorm chances to the area. Widespread rain chances \nshould continue into Thursday as the upper-low moves overhead \nbefore we dry out as everything moves off to the east by Friday.\nRainfall totals will be modest for most with less than 0.5 \ninch for many. A few locations over southeast Nebraska and \nsouthwest Iowa who see thunderstorms on Tuesday may receive \nupwards of an inch of rain.\n\nFriday sees surface high pressure settle into the Great Plains.\nTemperatures only reach the 50s with the brunt of the cold \nairmass moving in. Temperatures drop out Saturday morning as \n30s appear likely for the area. A potential first frost is on\nthe table by Saturday morning. Temperatures gradually warm back\ninto the 60s through the weekend with 70s beginning to return \nnext week. The weather remains quiet and dry otherwise. Enjoy \nthe Fall-like weather!",
